Both examples below start independently from the original before-state. Their resulting values are **contract-derived expectations, not executed results**.

For the safe rename, send this PATCH body:

```json
{
  "campaign": {
    "name": "campaigns/C7",
    "displayName": "Summer"
  },
  "updateMask": "displayName"
}
```

Expected stored resource:

```json
{
  "name": "campaigns/C7",
  "displayName": "Summer",
  "description": "Approved",
  "spendLimitMicros": "25000000"
}
```

For an intentional description reset, send:

```json
{
  "campaign": {
    "name": "campaigns/C7",
    "description": ""
  },
  "updateMask": "description"
}
```

Expected stored resource:

```json
{
  "name": "campaigns/C7",
  "displayName": "Spring",
  "description": "",
  "spendLimitMicros": "25000000"
}
```

Here the mask selects the reset target. If a generated serializer omits the empty description, the reset still follows from this provider’s selected-but-absent scalar rule, provided `"updateMask": "description"` reaches the provider. Omitting both description and the mask is destructive under this contract.
